    "encrypted": "false",
    "title": "Check Your Understanding",
    "long_instructions": "1. In your own words, explain the difference between a problem and an algorithm.\r\n2. What does sequencing mean, and why does the order of the steps matter in an algorithm?\r\n\r\nOptional sentence frame:\r\n>A problem is ______. An algorithm is ______.\r\n>\r\n>Sequencing means ______. The order of the steps matters because ______.",
    "stay_on_level_after_submit": "false",
    "height": "300",
    "allow_user_uploads": "false",
    "submittable": "true",
    "peer_reviewable": "false",
    "optional": "false",
    "allow_multiple_attempts": "false",
    "skip_dialog": true,
    "skip_sound": true,
    "name_suffix": "_2026",
    "teacher_markdown": "- **Look for a problem described as a general task.**\r\n  A strong response says a problem is a general description of a task, such as \"draw this trail,\" without listing steps.\r\n\r\n- **Look for an algorithm described as specific steps.**\r\n  A strong response says an algorithm is a finite set of instructions that solves the task, and that many different algorithms can solve the same problem.\r\n\r\n- **Push on why order matters.**\r\n  If students only write \"sequencing is putting steps in order,\" ask: \"What would change if you swapped two steps?\" Strong responses give an example, such as moving forward and then turning right drawing a different trail than turning right and then moving forward.\r\n\r\n- **Watch for mixed-up terms.**\r\n  If students describe a problem as a list of steps, or an algorithm as a goal, ask: \"Which one tells what to do, and which one tells how to do it?\""
  },
